<Doc>
    <p>Everything in this chapter is in <code>src/account</code> of the chapter app, beside
    <code>src/contacts</code> from chapter 1. Each card shows the files it is made of, and the
    test the app runs on them.</p>

    <Section title="`principal.set` signs a caller in">
        <Example
            id="crm-07-sign-in"
            app="crm-07"
            title="`principal.set` — sign in, sign out"
            summary="A team member signs in to the CRM by email, and signs out again."
            files={{
                'src/account/lib/SignIn.abide': signInComponentSource,
                'src/account/signIn.handler.ts': signInSource,
                'src/account/signOut.handler.ts': signOutSource,
                'src/account/lib/SignIn.test.ts': signInTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <SignIn />
        </Example>

        <p><code>principal.set(claims)</code> seals the claims into an <code>HttpOnly</code>
        cookie, and every request that carries it is that member's. <code>principal.clear()</code>
        signs them out. Both run in a handler, because the cookie is written on its answer. The
        <code>onPrincipal</code> at the foot of <code>signIn.handler.ts</code> is the next card's.</p>

        <p>The answer also tells the browser the caller changed. Every tab of the site reads
        <code>principal</code> again, so the form's own button flips with nothing passed to it.</p>

        <p>The seal is a JWT signed with <code>ABIDE_PRINCIPAL_SECRET</code>, and it lives for
        <code>ABIDE_PRINCIPAL_TTL</code> milliseconds, a week unless you set it. The chapter app
        keeps a development secret in its <code>.env</code>, so set your own wherever the app is
        deployed. A comma-separated list rotates a secret in: the first signs, and every one
        verifies. Under <code>bun test</code> an app that sets no secret is given one, so a test
        signs in from a fresh clone.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Read</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>principal.authenticated</code></td><td>whether this caller presented a seal the server accepted</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>principal.expiresAt</code></td><td>when the seal lapses</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>principal.resolved</code></td><td>who the caller is, as <code>onPrincipal</code> resolved them</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>principal.error</code></td><td>the resolver having failed, which leaves the caller signed out</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>principal.caller</code></td><td>which browser, signed in or not: a key for a rate limit or a per-device setting, never an identity</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Each is a reactive value. A template reading one follows it, and on the server each
        reads the request being answered.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`onPrincipal` turns the id in the cookie into a member">
        <Example
            id="crm-07-member"
            app="crm-07"
            title="`onPrincipal(fn)` — an id in the cookie, a member on the page"
            summary="The cookie holds a team member's id and nothing else. Sign in as either member and the page greets them by name and role."
            files={{
                'src/account/lib/Badge.abide': badgeSource,
                'src/account/signIn.handler.ts': signInSource,
                'src/account/lib/Badge.test.ts': badgeTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <Badge />
        </Example>

        <p><code>onPrincipal</code> is handed the claims and returns your half of the caller. It
        runs on the server, at most once per request, where the team records are. So the cookie
        stays small, and a rename shows on the next request rather than at the next sign-in.
        Filed in any handler file or <code>.abide</code>, it resolves the caller for every
        route.</p>

        <p>A test signs in without the cookie. <code>app.run(fn, &#123; principal: &#123; id: 'theo' &#125; &#125;)</code>
        hands those claims to <code>onPrincipal</code> as a sealed cookie's are, and the card's
        test reads the member they name.</p>

        <p>What it returns is read as <code>principal.resolved</code>, typed by
        <code>Principal</code>. Declare your fields on that interface, as the app's
        <code>lib/TEAM.server.ts</code> does beside the team it reads. abide's own three fields are <code>authenticated</code>, <code>expiresAt</code> and
        <code>error</code>, and they win any collision, so a resolver cannot sign a caller in by
        returning <code>authenticated: true</code>. A resolver that throws fills
        <code>error</code> and leaves the caller signed out.</p>

        <p>The tabs on this card, the deal-value card and the export card are
        <code>lib/SignInAs.abide</code>, which
        calls the same <code>signIn</code> handler.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`cookies()` reads and writes a cookie on both sides">
        <Example
            id="crm-07-density"
            app="crm-07"
            title="`cookies()` — one jar, read on both sides"
            summary="The contact list's density, kept in a cookie. Pick one and reload: the server paints the one you picked."
            files={{
                'src/account/lib/Density.abide': densitySource,
                'src/account/lib/Density.test.ts': densityTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <Density />
        </Example>

        <p><code>cookies()</code> is the request's cookies on the server and the document's in
        the browser, as a <code>CookieMap</code>. Reading a cookie by name follows it, so the
        list changes on the click. The server render reads the same cookie off the request, so a
        reload paints compact rather than flashing comfortable first.</p>

        <p><code>cookies()</code> reads and writes any cookie the request carries: a density, a
        theme, or another system's session cookie your server checks. The principal cookie is
        <code>HttpOnly</code>, so in the browser <code>cookies()</code> never sees the seal.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A handler decides which fields a caller may read">
        <Example
            id="crm-07-deal-value"
            app="crm-07"
            title="`principal.resolved` in a handler — the deal value, for admins"
            summary="Grace Hopper's contact card. Maya is an admin and sees the deal value; Theo is in sales and sees the rest."
            files={{
                'src/account/lib/ContactCard.abide': contactCardSource,
                'src/account/getContact.handler.ts': getContactSource,
                'src/account/lib/ContactCard.test.ts': contactCardTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ContactCard />
        </Example>

        <p>The handler reads <code>principal.resolved</code> and leaves the field out. Hiding it in
        the template would still send it, and anyone opening the network panel would read it.</p>

        <p>An answer read under one member is not read again on its own when another signs in, so
        the card watches <code>principal.resolved</code> and refreshes the contact. The test calls
        the handler in process, under the cookie each member's sign-in left.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A `middleware` rung refuses before the handler runs">
        <Example
            id="crm-07-admins-only"
            app="crm-07"
            title="`middleware: [rung]` — admins only"
            summary="Exporting every contact is for admins. Export signed out, as Theo, then as Maya."
            files={{
                'src/account/lib/ExportContacts.abide': exportContactsComponentSource,
                'src/account/exportContacts.handler.ts': exportContactsSource,
                'src/account/lib/ExportContacts.test.ts': exportContactsTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ExportContacts />
        </Example>

        <p>A rung is a <code>Middleware</code>: it is handed <code>next</code> and the call's
        context, and either returns <code>next(ctx)</code> or answers in the handler's place.
        Returning <code>refuse(403, …)</code> refuses, and neither the handler nor any rung after
        it runs. A throw escapes every rung rather than unwinding through them.</p>

        <p>The rung sits on the handler, so it runs on every call: from a browser, from another
        handler, and from a page rendering on the server. It reads arguments through
        <code>ctx.args()</code>, which parses the body on its first call. So a rung that never asks
        refuses a signed-out caller before their body is parsed or checked.</p>

        <p>To guard a whole section, call <code>middleware(…)</code> in its
        <code>layout.abide</code>, such as <code>src/account/admin/layout.abide</code>. That rung
        runs over every page and handler below it, and it has the request and nothing else:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`<script server>
import { principal } from '@abide/abide'
import { middleware } from '@abide/abide/server'

middleware(async (next) => {
    const { role } = await principal.resolved
    if (role !== 'admin') return new Response('Admins only.', { status: 403 })
    return next()
})
</script>

<slot/>`} />

        <p>Filed in the root <code>src/layout.abide</code>, a rung runs before routing, over
        every request the app answers. <code>abide routes</code> lists each rung beside the route
        it is filed at.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`crossOrigin` opens one handler to a partner">
        <Example
            id="crm-07-partner"
            app="crm-07"
            title="`crossOrigin: ['https://books.example']` — one partner, named back"
            summary="The bookkeeping partner's site reads an account statement from its own pages. Ask as the partner, then as any other site."
            files={{
                'src/account/lib/PartnerStatement.abide': partnerStatementSource,
                'src/account/getStatement.handler.ts': getStatementSource,
                'src/account/lib/PartnerStatement.test.ts': partnerStatementTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <PartnerStatement />
        </Example>

        <p>The answer names the partner in <code>access-control-allow-origin</code>, and the
        browser hands the statement only to a page from that origin. Every other handler in the
        CRM stays same-origin, because nothing widened it.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th><code>crossOrigin</code></th><th>Who may read the answer</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>absent</td><td>the app's own pages</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>['https://books.example']</code></td><td>those origins</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>true</code></td><td>any origin, with the caller's cookies, so keep it for answers that do not depend on who asked</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>abide answers the <code>OPTIONS</code> preflight a cross-origin <code>POST</code> sends,
        outside every rung. On a mutation, <code>crossOrigin</code> replaces the same-origin check
        a <code>POST</code> otherwise gets. Every rpc answer carries <code>vary: origin</code>, so
        a cache keeps one copy per asking origin. Pages carry
        <code>referrer-policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin</code>, so a link off the CRM does
        not hand another site the path a member was on.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`timeout` bounds how long a call may go without progress">
        <Example
            id="crm-07-timeout"
            app="crm-07"
            title="`timeout` — milliseconds without progress, not since the first byte"
            summary="Sales by region, a row every 300 ms, under a 500 ms timeout. North runs longer than the timeout and finishes; south stops producing after its first row."
            files={{
                'src/account/lib/SalesReport.abide': salesReportComponentSource,
                'src/account/salesReport.handler.ts': salesReportSource,
                'src/account/lib/SalesReport.test.ts': salesReportTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <SalesReport />
        </Example>

        <p>On a handler that yields, the bound is per row. So one number lets a long report run
        and still catches a source that has stopped. A call past it is refused
        <code>504</code>, and the rows already sent stay on the page.</p>

        <p>Without one, nothing is cut short: the server never closes a connection under a call it
        is still answering, a slow handler or a quiet stream alike. Its sibling is
        <code>maxBodySize</code>: the bytes a mutation's body may carry, refused <code>413</code>
        before a declared oversize body is read.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Default</th><th>What it bounds</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>ABIDE_RPC_TIMEOUT</code></td><td>none</td><td>the <code>timeout</code> of a handler that names none</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ABIDE_MAX_REQUEST_BODY_SIZE</code></td><td>none</td><td>the <code>maxBodySize</code> of a handler that names none</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ABIDE_MAX_SOCKET_MESSAGE_SIZE</code></td><td>1 MiB</td><td>one message a browser sends over the socket</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ABIDE_MAX_SOCKET_SUBSCRIPTIONS</code></td><td>1024</td><td>the values one connection may follow at once</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ABIDE_STOP_TIMEOUT</code></td><td>5000 ms</td><td>how long a stop waits on requests still being answered</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>None of these is a rate limit. A caller sending many small, fast requests passes every
        ceiling, and a rung keyed on <code>principal.caller</code> is where a rate limit goes.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`reachable` refuses at once while a host is down">
        <Example
            id="crm-07-billing"
            app="crm-07"
            title="`reachable(host)` — ask once, refuse at once"
            summary="Billing has stopped answering. Copy a contact to it twice: the first copy waits out the check, and the second is refused before it leaves."
            files={{
                'src/account/lib/SyncContact.abide': syncContactComponentSource,
                'src/account/syncContact.handler.ts': syncContactSource,
                'src/account/lib/SyncContact.test.ts': syncContactTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <SyncContact />
        </Example>

        <p><code>reachable</code> sends the host one <code>HEAD</code>, waits up to five seconds,
        and holds the answer for a minute for every caller in the process. A host that is down
        then costs one probe a minute instead of one timeout per call. Any answer counts, a
        <code>404</code> included: the question is whether something is there to call.</p>

        <p>The answer can be a minute old. The call that follows a <code>true</code> can still
        fail, and its failure is still the handler's to handle.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`response()` sets the status a handler answers with">
        <Example
            id="crm-07-created"
            app="crm-07"
            title="`response()` — a status the handler decides"
            summary="An integration adds contacts through the API, and expects 201 and the address of the new record."
            files={{
                'src/account/lib/AddContact.abide': addContactComponentSource,
                'src/account/addContact.handler.ts': addContactSource,
                'src/account/lib/AddContact.test.ts': addContactTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <AddContact />
        </Example>

        <p><code>response()</code> is the status and headers of the answer being built, before
        the body is decided. A handler or a rung writes to it and goes on returning a value.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it is, on the server, for the request being answered</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>request()</code></td><td>the platform's <code>Request</code>: headers, URL, method, signal</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>response()</code></td><td>the status and headers being sent back</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>server()</code></td><td>the listening <code>Bun.Server</code>, for what abide does not carry, such as a client's IP</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>On an rpc route the body is <code>ctx.args()</code>'s. A body reads once, so a rung that
        reads <code>request().json()</code> leaves nothing for the handler.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`csp()` lets the app's own scripts run, and nothing else">
        <Example
            id="crm-07-csp"
            app="crm-07"
            title="`csp()` — your own scripts run, a stranger's do not"
            summary="A support widget somebody pasted into the CRM from another origin. Add it, and the page's policy refuses it."
            files={{
                'src/account/lib/Widget.abide': widgetSource,
                'src/layout.abide': rootLayoutSource,
                'src/account/lib/Widget.test.ts': widgetTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <Widget />
        </Example>

        <p><code>csp()</code> is a rung, filed in the root layout so it covers every page. Its
        policy admits the build's own scripts and styles, and the inline script the render writes
        by a hash taken at build time. The widget's origin is not the app's, so the browser
        refuses it before a request is made. The docs app serves this page under the same
        baseline.</p>

        <p>Name a directive to replace it, such as <code>{`csp({ 'img-src': ["'self'", 'https://cdn.example'] })`}</code>
        for avatars on a CDN. The rest stay the baseline's.</p>

        <p>An inline script of your own reads <code>csp.nonce()</code>, the nonce this response
        carries, in the root layout:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`<script>
import { csp } from '@abide/abide'
</script>

<head>
    <script nonce={csp.nonce()}>window.dataLayer = []</script>
</head>`} />

        <p>Without <code>csp()</code>, a page is still served
        <code>content-security-policy: frame-ancestors 'none'</code>, so no other site can frame
        it and steer a click.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/guide/run-it')}>Run it</a> — configure the CRM, start and stop it,
            and see what it is doing</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
